<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boun Oum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Boun Oum na Champassak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boun Oum was the hereditary prince of Champasak in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. In 1946 he renounced his dynastic claims so that Laos could be unified under the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Phrabang</a>, and in return became inspector general of the kingdom. He was a leading right-wing politician and served as prime minister of the Kingdom of Laos in 1949–1950 and 1960–1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boun-oum.jpg" alt="Boun Oum" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boun_Oum_1971.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
